+// sandbox cannot have virtual time at all: under a service worker the load
+// being waited on is served by the worker, whose transport needs timers, so
+// neither side ever moves (RULES.md #59). That left the sandbox on the real
+// wall clock while the oracle ran from a pinned origin -- a difference the
+// GUEST can read, and not only as a fingerprint. Cloudflare's JS detections
+// take the challenge's issue time out of `__CF$cv$params.t` and compare it
+// against `Date.now()`; replaying a recorded challenge hours later makes it
+// look stale, and the script stops without an error. Measured: the oracle
+// posted 16270 bytes to `jsd/oneshot` and the sandbox posted nothing, having
+// executed exactly six operations.
+//
+// So: offset, not virtual. `base::Time::Now()` is shifted by a delta fixed at
+// startup and then runs at real speed, which is what a sandbox needs -- the
+// same origin as the oracle, a clock that still advances on its own. TimeTicks
+// is deliberately untouched: it is monotonic-since-boot, has no epoch to
+// agree on, and is what `performance.now()` measures.
